      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">the following references, each of which is incorporated herein by reference,</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">illustrate the state of the art for making membranes having a thickness greater than 1 mil: U.S. Pat.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">the relevant quench conditions for a standard film process</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">include: an air pressure of about 6&#34; H 2 O; an air ring gap in the range of 10/64 to 15/64 inches; and an air ring height of 1 to 2 inches; on the other hand, the relevant quench conditions of a thin film process include: an air pressure of about 0.6 to 3.0&#34; H 2 O; an air ring gap in the range of 5/64 to 10/64 inches; and a ring height of about 1 to 2 inches.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">the relevant extrusion conditions for a standard film process</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">include: a die temperature in the range of 191 to 198° C. and a barrel temperature of 200 to 205° C.; on the other hand, the relevant extrusion conditions for a thin film process (using the same material) include: a die temperature in the range of 210° C. (for 0.5 mil final product) to 224° C. (for 0.33 mil final product) and a barrel temperature of 210° C.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="">
      </li>
      <li itemprop="definitions" itemscope repeat>
        <span itemprop="subject">the inter-ply adhesion</span>
        <span itemprop="definition">(measured as peel strength) must be lower than that of the standard process, so that the individual plies do not split (i.e. tear apart) when they are deplied.</span>
        <meta itemprop="num_attr" content="">
